{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Foundations of CBI and Design Tensions", "title": "Teacher Agency With Technology", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting teacher agency with technology to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "01", "section": "06", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/01/05"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_1_6_teacher_agency_with_technology_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_6_teacher_agency_with_technology_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_6_teacher_agency_with_technology_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_1_6_teacher_agency_with_technology_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 6, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Teacher Agency With Technology\n\n![Teacher Agency With Technology textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/01-06-teacher-agency-with-technology.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen a tool is mandated, it is tempting to believe the decisions have all been made elsewhere and your only options are compliance or quiet sabotage. That belief is both false and consequential, because a mandated platform still has to land in a real room with real students, and how it lands is almost always within your control. Agency is not the freedom to refuse the mandate; it is the authority and the responsibility for the part of the decision that remains yours.\n\nThis section matters because the most common failures with mandated technology are failures of abdication, not defiance. A teacher who runs the default sequence unchanged has not \"followed policy\"; she has handed the sequencing of student learning to the platform's logic and stopped being the professional in the loop. Teacher agency is the disciplined practice of adapting and justifying deviations within a constraint, documenting them, and feeding evidence back into the policy so the constraint itself can improve.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA district mandates an adaptive math platform that auto-assigns each student a next lesson. A teacher notices it routes her struggling readers past a fractions-vocabulary step they need, but the platform is required, so her first instinct is to let it assign whatever it assigns and treat any struggle as the platform's design, not her call. That instinct quietly surrenders the one decision that was still hers: how the mandate lands for these specific students.\n\nProfessional judgment becomes visible the moment she stops asking \"am I allowed to change this?\" and starts asking \"where, inside this mandate, do I still have authority, and what does the evidence tell me to do with it?\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nTeacher agency with technology is the capacity to make and justify decisions within a constraint, rather than the freedom to remove the constraint. It lives in the gap between blind compliance (running the default and disowning the result) and covert refusal (ignoring the mandate without record). Both extremes give up agency; one to the platform, the other to the shadows.\n\nExercising agency well has a recognizable structure:\n\n- **Locate the surrendered decision.** Identify what you handed to the tool by accepting its default. Sequencing, pacing, grouping, what counts as \"done.\" Naming it is the precondition for reclaiming it.\n- **Find the specific harm with evidence.** Agency without evidence is just preference reasserting itself. Tie the deviation to a measured effect on named students.\n- **Act within the bounds.** Make the smallest justified adaptation that addresses the harm while honoring the mandate, and log the deviation.\n- **Feed evidence upward.** Bring the data to whoever owns the policy so the constraint can be revised. Agency includes acting on the policy, not only inside it.\n\nDocumentation is what separates agency from refusal: a logged, evidence-backed deviation is a professional act a colleague can inspect, while an unlogged one is indistinguishable from non-compliance. Critical edtech studies names the harm a default can cause; [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) keeps the adaptation tied to the content goal; datafication reminds you that the platform's routing logic is itself a designed artifact, not a fact of nature.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher quietly ignores a mandated platform's sequence for some students but writes nothing down. Is this an exercise of teacher agency?\" explanation=\"No. An unlogged, unevidenced deviation is indistinguishable from non-compliance; it forfeits documentation and the chance to change the policy. Agency is a logged, evidence-backed deviation a colleague can inspect, not covert refusal.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, any deviation from a mandate is agency\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No; an unlogged deviation is refusal, not agency\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, because the students benefit\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, because teachers may never deviate from a mandate\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nScenario: the teacher from the opening case decides what exercising agency looks like inside the math-platform mandate. Walk it in four numbered moves.\n\n**Before (compliance-only framing).** Her note reads:\n\n> The platform is required, so I let it assign whatever it assigns. If students struggle, that is the platform's design, not my call.\n\n1. **Locate the lost agency.** By running the default sequence unchanged, she has handed the sequencing decision to the platform's logic, including the skipped vocabulary step.\n2. **Find the specific harm with evidence.** Over two weeks, the 6 students the platform fast-tracked scored 40 percent on the fractions-vocabulary quiz, versus 80 percent for the rest of the class.\n3. **Exercise bounded agency within the mandate.** She keeps the platform as required but inserts a 10-minute vocabulary mini-lesson before the auto-assigned task for those 6 students, and logs the change.\n4. **Feed evidence back into the policy.** She brings the 40-vs-80 gap to her grade team to request that the platform's prerequisite-skip setting be disabled district-wide.\n\n**After (agency-within-constraint framing).** The revised note reads:\n\n> I use the mandated platform but I am the author of how it lands. Evidence: it skipped a fractions-vocabulary step and the 6 fast-tracked students scored 40 percent vs. 80 percent for the class. I inserted a 10-minute vocabulary step for those students and submitted the gap data to my team to get the skip setting changed. I documented every deviation.\n\nThe decision is defensible because agency is shown as the capacity to adapt and justify deviations within the mandate, backed by evidence, rather than as either blind compliance or covert refusal.\n\nWork the mandate as a live dilemma. Steer between blind compliance and covert refusal: reclaim the decision the default made, back it with evidence, and push it upward.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Inside the mandated adaptive math platform\",\"start\":\"m1\",\"nodes\":{\"m1\":{\"prompt\":\"The required platform auto-assigns lessons and routes your struggling readers past a fractions-vocabulary step. The platform is mandatory. What is your stance?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Run the default; any struggle is the platform design, not my call\",\"next\":\"mComply\",\"feedback\":\"Blind compliance. You handed the sequencing decision to the platform and disowned the result.\"},{\"label\":\"Ignore the platform for those students and teach my own sequence\",\"next\":\"mRefuse\",\"feedback\":\"Covert refusal. Without a record, this is indistinguishable from non-compliance and cannot change the policy.\"},{\"label\":\"Locate the decision the default made and gather evidence on the harm\",\"next\":\"mLocate\",\"feedback\":\"This is where agency lives: reclaim the sequencing decision the default quietly took.\"}]},\"mComply\":{\"prompt\":\"Six fast-tracked students score 40 percent on the fractions-vocabulary quiz vs. 80 percent for the class. You attribute it to the platform. What did you give up?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Nothing; the platform owns sequencing\",\"next\":\"mLoss\",\"feedback\":\"You surrendered the one decision that was still yours: how the mandate lands for these students.\"},{\"label\":\"The authority to adapt how the mandate lands for these 6 students\",\"next\":\"mLocate\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Reclaim it with a bounded, logged adaptation.\"}]},\"mRefuse\":{\"prompt\":\"Your workaround helps, but there is no record and the district never learns the skip setting is harmful. What is the cost?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"None; the students are fine\",\"next\":\"mLoss\",\"feedback\":\"The policy stays broken for every other class, and your act cannot be defended or audited.\"},{\"label\":\"You lose documentation and the chance to fix the policy district-wide\",\"next\":\"mLocate\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. Convert the workaround into a logged, evidenced deviation routed upward.\"}]},\"mLocate\":{\"prompt\":\"Over two weeks the 6 fast-tracked students score 40 percent vs. 80 percent. What is the smallest justified deviation?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Drop the platform entirely for the whole class\",\"next\":\"mLoss\",\"feedback\":\"Larger than the harm requires and abandons the mandate. Make the smallest bounded adaptation.\"},{\"label\":\"Insert a 10-minute vocabulary mini-lesson for the 6, log it, and send the gap data to the grade team\",\"next\":\"mWin\",\"feedback\":\"Bounded, evidenced, logged, and routed to the policy owner to disable the skip setting.\"}]},\"mWin\":{\"prompt\":\"You authored how the mandate landed: a logged 10-minute step justified by the 40-vs-80 gap, with the data sent upward to change the skip setting.\",\"feedback\":\"This is agency as accountable authorship inside a constraint.\",\"choices\":[]},\"mLoss\":{\"prompt\":\"You either disowned the result or acted without a record, so a colleague cannot defend the call and the policy stays unchanged.\",\"feedback\":\"Revisit: smallest bounded deviation, evidenced, logged, routed upward.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nInside a mandate, name the value the default is harming first, weigh the affordance second, and confirm the policy boundary third so your deviation stays within bounds. A tool choice, or a tool adaptation, is justified only when it protects students while improving a specific learning activity, and the deviation is documented.\n\nA defensible exercise of agency survives three quick tests:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** What decision did the default quietly make for you, and is reclaiming it in service of a learning goal?\n2. **Evidence check:** What measured harm to named students justifies the deviation, rather than a hunch?\n3. **Revision check:** Did you log the deviation and route the evidence to whoever can change the policy?\n\nIf the deviation is unlogged or unevidenced, it is refusal, not agency.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"The Transfer Case notes that when a teacher freely chose a tool (no mandate), the 'locate the surrendered decision' step disappears but the evidence and documentation steps remain. Explain why agency still requires evidence and a record even with no mandate to push back against.\" answer=\"Agency is accountable authorship, not merely resistance to authority. With no mandate, there is no default someone else set, so nothing was surrendered to reclaim. But the teacher's own free choice still affects named students and still has to be defensible to a reviewer, so it must rest on measured evidence rather than intuition, and it must be documented so a colleague can inspect and challenge it. If your reasoning only disciplines decisions made against a mandate, you learned to push back; agency means the same evidence-and-record discipline governs your own free choices too.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first error is equating agency with refusal, as if the only autonomous act is to ignore the mandate; this forfeits documentation and the chance to change the policy. The second is the mirror image, reading a mandate as total, so that any struggle becomes \"the platform's fault\"; this surrenders the decisions that genuinely remained yours. A third is exercising agency on intuition alone; a deviation with no evidence is not professional judgment, it is preference wearing a lab coat, and a colleague cannot defend it for you.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the structure to a new mandate: a school requires all feedback to go through an AI comment-bank that inserts canned praise and correction phrases. A teacher feels the comments miss her students' actual reasoning.\n\nLocate the surrendered decision. By accepting the default phrases, she has handed the substance of feedback, what students hear about their thinking, to a generic bank. That is the authority to reclaim.\n\nFind the harm with evidence. She reviews a week of bot comments on her 5 advanced writers and finds the canned phrases praised surface neatness while ignoring the argument flaws she had flagged in class; two students then doubled down on weak claims, citing the bot's praise.\n\nAct within bounds and feed upward. She keeps the comment-bank for routine mechanics but appends a personal note on argument quality for essays where the bank misses the point, logging each override. She brings the two-student example to her department to request that the bank allow teacher-authored comments to take precedence. The walkthrough shows the same agency structure on a feedback mandate: name what the default decided, evidence the harm, deviate within bounds with a record, and push the evidence toward the policy.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: the **decision the default made for the teacher** on the left, the **evidence and bounded deviation that reclaim it** on the right. For this topic, the tell that you are reading well is that you can distinguish a logged, evidenced deviation from an unlogged one and explain why only the first counts as agency.\n\nA strong reader of teacher agency can answer four questions:\n\n1. What decision did accepting the default hand to the tool?\n2. What measured harm to which students justifies reclaiming it?\n3. What is the smallest deviation that addresses the harm while honoring the mandate?\n4. How was the deviation documented and routed back to the policy owner?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when it shows a reclaimed decision, the evidence of harm that justified it, a bounded and logged deviation, and a path that carries the evidence back to the policy. An artifact that only complies, or only refuses, has skipped the agency entirely; mark that gap and ask BigAL to compare a \"ran the default\" note against a \"documented bounded deviation\" note rather than for a general explanation.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like: \"I use the mandated platform but author how it lands; the 40-vs-80 gap justified a 10-minute vocabulary step for 6 students, logged, with the gap data sent to my team to change the skip setting.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the agency question into a setting with no mandate at all, where the teacher freely chose the tool. The structure inverts: there is no default someone else set, so the surrendered-decision step disappears, but the evidence and documentation steps remain, now aimed at justifying her own free choice to a reviewer. If your reasoning only works against an external mandate, you learned to push back on authority; if it also disciplines your own free choices, you understood agency as accountable authorship.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/01-06-teacher-agency-with-technology-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"01.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 01.06. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how bounded, documented agency connects to evidence-based revision and the course anchors.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions\", \"cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_teacher_agency_with_technology_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology", "stem": "An adaptive platform automatically assigns each student a 'next lesson.' A teacher follows the platform's sequence without change all term. Which statement best describes the state of the teacher's agency?", "options": ["Agency is maximized because the teacher is using advanced technology.", "Agency has been ceded to the platform's logic; the teacher exercises agency only when she overrides or adapts the sequence based on what she knows about her students.", "Agency is irrelevant once a platform is adopted.", "Agency means the teacher must reject the platform entirely."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Teacher agency is the capacity to make and justify deviations, not the act of running a tool as configured. Following the default sequence unchanged hands the instructional decisions to the platform.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_using_tech_equals_agency", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_teacher_agency_with_technology_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology", "stem": "A district mandates a scripted AI lesson tool. A teacher notices it skips a step her struggling readers need. Which response best exercises professional agency within the constraint?", "options": ["Quietly stop using the tool and tell no one.", "Use the tool as mandated but document where it underserves her readers, insert the missing step, and bring that evidence to her team to request a revision.", "Follow the script exactly even where it harms students, since it is mandated.", "Complain to students about the district."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Agency is not all-or-nothing defiance. It is working within a mandate while documenting harm, adapting where a professional judgment requires it, and feeding evidence back into the policy.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_mandate_eliminates_agency", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_teacher_agency_with_technology_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "An AI lesson planner generates a full unit. A teacher who wants to preserve her agency should treat the output as:", "options": ["A finished unit to deliver as written, since the AI optimized it.", "A draft she interrogates against her students and curriculum, accepting, cutting, or rewriting parts and keeping a record of what she changed and why.", "A reason to stop planning her own units permanently.", "Proof that lesson planning no longer requires a teacher."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Preserving agency with AI means staying the author of record: the teacher interrogates and revises the draft and can account for every retained or rejected element, rather than delivering generated output unexamined.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_ai_authorship_replaces_teacher", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_teacher_agency_with_technology_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology", "stem": "A teacher says 'I have full agency because I can turn the AI tutor off whenever I want.' Why is an on/off switch an incomplete account of agency?", "options": ["Because off switches are unreliable.", "Because meaningful agency is the ongoing capacity to shape HOW the tool operates (its content, pacing, and limits) for her students, not only the binary power to abandon it.", "Because she should never turn the tool off.", "Because agency is only about who bought the tool."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A binary on/off is the weakest form of agency. Real agency is the capacity to configure, override, and direct the tool's behavior in service of specific students, exercised continuously rather than once.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_agency_as_onoff_switch", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_teacher_agency_with_technology", "evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "c5131fae7e4ecc64d72d6b03965ddd064386e3494010f8bd56d60e2803abc22b", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_using_tech_equals_agency", "pattern": "tool_use_equated_with_agency", "description": "Believing that using advanced technology is itself an exercise of teacher agency, even when the teacher follows the tool's defaults unchanged", "trigger": "learner equates running a platform as configured with exercising professional agency", "feedback": "Agency lives in the deviations you make and can justify, not in operating the tool. Identify where you override or adapt the default for your students.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_mandate_eliminates_agency", "pattern": "mandate_read_as_zero_agency", "description": "Treating a district mandate as eliminating all professional agency, leaving only full compliance or covert refusal", "trigger": "learner sees only obey-exactly or quietly-quit when a tool is mandated", "feedback": "Agency within a mandate means documenting harm, adapting where judgment requires, and feeding evidence back to revise the policy. It is not binary.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_ai_authorship_replaces_teacher", "pattern": "ai_output_delivered_unexamined", "description": "Delivering AI-generated lesson material as written, ceding authorship and the accountability that comes with it", "trigger": "learner treats an AI-generated unit as finished rather than a draft to interrogate and revise", "feedback": "Stay the author of record. Interrogate the draft against your students and curriculum, and keep a record of what you changed and why.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_6_agency_as_onoff_switch", "pattern": "agency_reduced_to_on_off", "description": "Reducing teacher agency to the binary power to turn a tool on or off rather than the capacity to shape how it operates", "trigger": "learner claims full agency solely because the tool can be disabled", "feedback": "Go beyond the on/off switch. Real agency is shaping the tool's content, pacing, and limits for specific students, exercised continuously.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}